1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.100
Dynamic Data Export offers three chart types, 

2
00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:05.300
Table, Line, and Pie.

3
00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:07.500
But how do you know which one to choose?

4
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:08.766
Let's break it down.

5
00:00:08.766 --> 00:00:12.566
Tables are your go-to for detailed, precise data.

6
00:00:12.566 --> 00:00:15.232
Use tables when you need to see exact numbers, 

7
00:00:15.233 --> 00:00:17.366
compare multiple values side by side,

8
00:00:17.366 --> 00:00:20.766
or when your audience needs to reference specific data points.

9
00:00:20.766 --> 00:00:24.966
Tables also support the most dimensions and filtering options.

10
00:00:26.333 --> 00:00:29.899
Line charts are perfect for spotting trends over time.

11
00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:33.033
Use them when you want to see how something changes. 

12
00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:35.933
Like battery levels each day

13
00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:39.233
or app usage patterns week over week.

14
00:00:41.566 --> 00:00:44.699
Line charts make it easy to identify increases,

15
00:00:44.966 --> 00:00:46.032
decreases,

16
00:00:46.366 --> 00:00:48.399
and patterns at a glance.

17
00:00:48.833 --> 00:00:51.533
Pie charts show proportions and percentages. 

18
00:00:51.533 --> 00:00:54.933
They're ideal when you want to show how parts relate to a whole,

19
00:00:54.933 --> 00:00:58.899
like the percentage of battery drain attributed to different apps,

20
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.666
or the distribution of device models in your fleet.

21
00:01:03.433 --> 00:01:04.433
Keep in mind,

22
00:01:04.433 --> 00:01:08.499
Pie charts work best with your data points for clarity.

23
00:01:08.966 --> 00:01:10.466
Here's a quick summary,

24
00:01:10.466 --> 00:01:13.799
tables for detail, lines for trends,

25
00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.066
and pies for proportions.

26
00:01:16.533 --> 00:01:21.099
The same data can tell different stories, depending on how you present it.

27
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.066
Now that you know which chart to use,

28
00:01:24.366 --> 00:01:26.199
let's talk about filters.

29
00:01:26.233 --> 00:01:30.599
Your tool for zeroing in on exactly the data you need.

30
00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:32.966
See you in the next video.

